  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nEarly morning patchy ground fog has dissipated and sunny skies \ndominate across the region as high pressure settles over the area. \nTroughing is exiting the region to the east and ridging will begin \nto build in. The ridge axis extends from southern California into \nthe Northern Rockies placing the Sunflower state under upper level \nnorthwesterly flow. At the surface, a faint boundary is draped \nacross the KS/NE border. This feature will begin lifting northward \nas a warm front later today. Southerly to southeasterly flow will \nsupport a gradual warmup across the area beginning today and \ncontinuing into the middle of the week. Highs each afternoon through \nWednesday will top out in the upper 60s and lower 70s with overnight \nlows generally falling into the 40s areawide. Mostly clear \nconditions are expected, however overnight radiational cooling may \ncontinue to lead to patchy fog across the state each morning. \n\nBy mid week, a deep trough will begin to dig into the Northeast US \nThe forecast area will on the western periphery of the trough and \nwill be grazed by multiple short waves for the end of the week. The \nbetter dynamics and moisture look to remain just to our east at this \ntime, limiting our precip chances with this feature. A cold front \nwill push across the eastern half of the country bringing more \nseasonable temperatures to the Central Plains. Temperatures for \nThursday and Friday will be slightly cooler with highs topping out \nin the upper 50s and lower 60s and lows dipping into the 30s once \nagain.",
